    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d91">

                <pb facs="0136" n="126" sameAs="frus:frus1945v07/pg126"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.014/7–445</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by Mr. Eugene H. Dooman, Special Assistant to
                        the Assistant Secretary of State</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Dunn</hi>),
                        <hi rendition="#italic">to the Director of the Office of Far Eastern
                        Affairs</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Ballantine</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1945-07-04">July 4, 1945</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>With reference to Mr. Grew’s memoranda of May 14, 1945 summarizing a conversation
                    between the President and T. V. Soong, it will be recalled that there was brief
                    mention made of the retrocession to China of Chinese areas taken by Japan.</p>
                <p>I have ascertained from Mr. Grew that nothing in the relevant portion of his
                    memoranda should be construed as an acceptance by the President of any new
                    commitment. The statement of the President with regard to Manchuria and Formosa
                    should be read as a reaffirmation—and nothing more—of our intention to carry out
                    the terms of the Cairo Declaration.<anchor n="57" xml:id="d91-f1"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">E. H. Dooman</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d91-f1" n="57" xml:id="d91-f1-content">
                <p>See White House press release of December 1, 1943, Department of State <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Bulletin</hi>, December 4, 1943, p. 393, and <ref
                        target="frus1943CairoTehran#pg_448">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, The Conferences at Cairo and
                        Tehran, 1943, p. 448</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
